  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 157 PM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nThe cumulus field that was present early this morning has shown no \nsigns of dissipating with expansive cumulus across all of WI and \ninto much of MN, with the clearing line stretching from roughly \nAlexandria south through Willmar and towards Redwood Falls as of \naround 2pm. Forecast soundings show enough saturation from 5-7kft \nthat as long as we continue to mix this field of clouds is unlikely \nto change, meaning we will probably seem them lingering through 8-\n9pm before beginning to dissipate. The overnight hours will be \nuneventful with light winds and clearing skies, with some patchy fog \npossible mainly in western WI from roughly 4am through 9am. Tomorrow \nwill be similar to today but with near normal temperatures in the \nlow 80s amidst partly cloudy skies with fair weather cumulus once \nagain. \n\nWe warm up significantly by Saturday as another western CONUS ridge \nbegins to strengthen with rising heights centered over Colorado \nresulting in a similar pattern to our last heat wave, as highs look \nto reach the low 90s by Saturday and remain in the 90s into early \nnext week. There is still some fairly significant spread in the \ngreater ensemble guidance for temperatures throughout next week, \nwith the ECMWF/EPS on the hotter side compared to the GFS/GEFS, with \nthe primary difference being how far northeast the ridge ends up. \nBoth sets of guidance would favor heights at 500mb building towards \nthat 600dm mark that we reached with our last heat wave, and as of \nthe 12/18z runs suggest 597dm heights at its peak for now. The \nGFS/GEFS keep the ridge a tad farther west, which lines up \nnorthwesterly flow at 500mb across the area and gives us a chance \nfor ridge rider shortwaves which could produce some isolated to \nscattered convection, which would eat into our temperatures via \nprecipitation or cloud cover. The ECMWF/EPS produce a slightly \nstronger ridge and also place it far east, which would result in \nlower chances for storms and clearer skies, resulting in warmer \ntemperatures. The NBM took the middle ground and keeps us in the low \nto mid 90s through Monday before dropping back to upper 80s to near \n90 for the rest of next week, which seems like a happy medium for \nnow. Most guidance would suggest continued above normal temperatures \nthrough the rest of the month and into early August, with the main \ndifference being are we around 5 degrees above normal like the GEFS \nor 10-15 degrees above normal like the EPS. The forecast \ntemperatures are by no means locked in but being above normal is all \nbut a certainty at this point.",
